      "problem": "Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe integer $9$ \n{{field-1}}\nthe additive inverse of $16$ modulo $25$ since $9+16$ {{field-2}} $(\\text{mod}\\,25).$",
      "worked_solution": "The additive inverse of $16$ modulo $25$ is the integer $0\\le x<25$ such that\n\n$$\nx+16≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that when we add our two numbers, we get a number that is congruent to zero modulo $25:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9+16 & ≡25 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,25) \\\\  & ≡0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude the following:\n\nThe integer $9$ $\\text{is}$ the additive inverse of $16$ modulo $25$ since $9+16≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,25).$",
